---
import MainLayout from '../layouts/MainLayout.astro';
import ServiceEvidencePanel from '../components/ServiceEvidencePanel.astro';
import serviceEvidence from '../data/serviceEvidence.json';
import LegacyHeader from '../components/LegacyHeader.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import ContactSection from '../components/ContactSection.astro';
import Hero from '../components/Hero.astro';
import ButtonPrimary from '../components/ButtonPrimary.astro';
import ButtonSecondary from '../components/ButtonSecondary.astro';

const faqs = [
  {
    question: 'How much do SEO services cost in the UK?',
    answer: "We publish no rate card, because the fee follows the work. Your GBP proposal is built around the pages, the technical work and the review support your site needs. It separates research, writing, implementation and reporting, lists what is excluded and states the payment terms. A local service site and a national catalogue are different workloads, so they are priced differently. Compare proposals on the work they include before you compare the monthly figure."
  },
  {
    question: 'How long does SEO take to work in the UK market?',
    answer: "The first stage is groundwork: the audit, the technical fixes and the content foundations. After that, the timing depends on your market, the state of the site and the agreed scope. Our published UK comparisons give a sense of the periods involved: three months against the same months a year earlier for Emily Bridalwear, and six months against the previous six for Conscient, ThermoPest and Phoenix Brighton Spa. We report against dated evidence of that kind. We do not promise a fixed result by a fixed date."
  },
  {
    question: 'Can you guarantee page 1 rankings on Google?',
    answer: "No. Nobody outside Google controls rankings, and an agency that guarantees a position is selling something it cannot deliver. We agree the work, the review points and the measures of progress. Then we review UK query and landing-page performance beside the completed changes, including the periods where results do not improve. Our case studies show the same habit: Phoenix Brighton Spa gained clicks while its average position and click-through rate got worse, and the case study says so."
  },
  {
    question: 'What does a monthly SEO retainer actually include?',
    answer: "A typical month covers technical monitoring and fixes, on-page optimisation, British English content production or briefs, UK-relevant link outreach, and a plain-English report showing rankings, traffic, work completed and next steps. The mix shifts with the phase of the campaign: month one is audit-heavy, and later months lean into content and authority. The proposal lists what is in scope for you. You keep full access to your own Google Analytics and Search Console data throughout."
  },
  {
    question: 'Why would a UK business use an SEO agency in the Philippines?',
    answer: "Because the team is British-led and its UK work is published for you to inspect. Our founder, Ben Lowe, has worked in SEO for more than 15 years and holds a marketing degree from London Metropolitan University. The delivery team is in Quezon City and covers technical review, British English content and implementation support. Compare our proposed responsibilities, our UK case studies and our review windows with the capacity your team needs. The Emily Bridalwear, Conscient and ThermoPest cases are the quickest way to inspect the work."
  },
  {
    question: 'How do the time zones work between the UK and the Philippines?',
    answer: "Manila is eight hours ahead of GMT and seven ahead of BST, and the Philippines has no daylight saving. A 9:00 UK call is 17:00 in Manila on GMT or 16:00 on BST. Our office hours are Monday to Friday, 9:00 to 18:00 Manila time, so our day ends at 10:00 GMT or 11:00 BST. That leaves a live overlap at the start of your morning, with written handovers for everything else. We agree meeting windows and delivery dates around the work and your approval process."
  },
  {
    question: 'Will my content be written in British English?',
    answer: "Yes. We write in British English and check terminology against your products and your customers. For a UK store, that means more than spelling: the page carries the actual GBP prices, delivery coverage and returns information supplied by your team. Drafts are edited by a person before they reach you, and your team approves commercial claims before publication."
  },
  {
    question: 'Can you build links from UK websites?',
    answer: "Yes. Link relevance matters for country-level rankings, so UK campaigns look first at UK-relevant sources: publications, industry directories, supplier and partner sites, and digital PR angles that make sense to a British audience. We review each prospect for relevance before outreach. We do not sell bulk link packages, and we will show you where every link comes from."
  },
  {
    question: 'Do you tie clients into long contracts?',
    answer: "There is no standard contract. The engagement length, the payment terms and any notice period are written into your proposal, where you can read them before committing. Before you agree a retainer with us or anyone else, confirm three things: the work included, the review schedule, and which files and account access are handed over at the end."
  },
  {
    question: 'How do you measure and report SEO results?',
    answer: "From your own data. Search Console shows query and landing-page performance, and GA4 shows recorded site activity. We agree the UK segments, the comparison periods and the conversion definitions at the start, then report the completed work, the dated comparison and the next priorities. Searches made in the UK are kept separate from queries that merely mention the UK. Rank tracking or AI monitoring is included where the scope says so."
  },
  {
    question: 'Does SEO still work now that people use ChatGPT and AI Overviews?',
    answer: "Yes. Search work still starts with pages buyers can find and use, and AI answers draw on the same pages. AI-search work adds three checks: the product facts on the site, the answers to buying questions and a defined sample of prompts. We report observed mentions separately from referral sessions, and we confirm the event definition before treating a tracked action as a signup, an order or an enquiry. Four of our published AI referral reviews cover UK-market businesses, including Newbury Self Store and Emily Bridalwear."
  },
  {
    question: 'Should I hire a UK agency, a freelancer or an offshore team?',
    answer: "Match the provider to the job. A freelancer often suits one focused task. A team suits work that spans technical SEO, content and implementation. A UK agency is the right call if you need people in the room. Whichever you shortlist, ask who delivers and who reviews each part, what your own team must provide, and how changes are checked before release. Then ask for the office hours in UK time. Ours end at 10:00 GMT or 11:00 BST."
  }
];
---

<MainLayout
  title="SEO Services for UK Businesses | Bright Forge"
  description="SEO for UK businesses from a British-led team in the Philippines. Published UK case studies, British English content and proposals priced in sterling."
  canonical="https://brightforge.com.ph/seo-services-uk/"
  type="service"
  includeServiceSchema={false}
  faqs={faqs}
  geoRegion="GB"
  geoPlacename="United Kingdom"
  contentLanguage="en-GB"
>
  <Fragment slot="head">
    <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
      '@context': 'https://schema.org',
      '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
      itemListElement: [
        {
          '@type': 'ListItem',
          position: 1,
          name: 'Home',
          item: 'https://brightforge.com.ph/'
        },
        {
          '@type': 'ListItem',
          position: 2,
          name: 'SEO Services UK',
          item: 'https://brightforge.com.ph/seo-services-uk/'
        }
      ]
    })} />
    <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
      '@context': 'https://schema.org',
      '@type': 'Service',
      name: 'SEO Services UK',
      serviceType: 'Search engine optimisation',
      url: 'https://brightforge.com.ph/seo-services-uk/',
      areaServed: {
        '@type': 'Country',
        name: 'United Kingdom'
      },
      availableLanguage: 'en-GB',
      provider: {
        '@type': 'Organization',
        name: 'Bright Forge SEO',
        url: 'https://brightforge.com.ph/'
      },
      description: 'Search engine optimisation services for UK businesses: technical SEO, British English content, UK-relevant link building, AI search optimisation and transparent monthly reporting.'
    })} />
  </Fragment>

  <LegacyHeader slot="header" />

  <Hero
    badgeIcon="fas fa-globe-europe"
    badgeText="SEO for UK Businesses"
    title="SEO Services for UK Businesses With <span>Work You Can Verify</span>"
    breadcrumbCurrent="SEO Services UK"
  >
    <Fragment slot="intro">
      <p>Our published UK case studies cover a bridal retailer, a B2B consultancy, a pest control firm and a Brighton spa, each with its Search Console figures. We help UK ecommerce brands, SaaS products and service businesses win visibility on google.co.uk with technical SEO, British English content, UK-relevant links and reporting you can verify in your own analytics.</p>
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="story">
      <p>We are a British-led team delivering from Quezon City in the Philippines, writing in British English for UK buyers. Your proposal identifies the pages we will work on, who implements the changes and how your team reviews them.</p>
    </Fragment>

    <Fragment slot="actions">
      <ButtonPrimary href="/contact/?service=seo-services&market=UK#contactForm" text="Get a UK SEO Proposal" />
      <ButtonSecondary href="#uk-results" text="See UK Project Work" />
    </Fragment>

    <Fragment slot="visual">
      <div class="market-visual" aria-label="UK SEO campaign snapshot">
        <div class="market-node">
          <span>Your market</span>
          <strong>google.co.uk, AI search, UK buyers and competitors</strong>
        </div>
        <div class="market-line"></div>
        <div class="market-node forge">
          <span>Your SEO team</span>
          <strong>British-led, named specialists in the Philippines</strong>
        </div>
        <div class="market-line"></div>
        <div class="market-node">
          <span>Your advantage</span>
          <strong>Agreed delivery dates and UK review windows</strong>
        </div>
      </div>
    </Fragment>
  </Hero>

  <section class="proof-strip" aria-label="UK SEO proof points">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="proof-grid">
        <div><strong>+137.5% organic clicks</strong><span>Emily Bridalwear, 3 months year on year</span></div>
        <div><strong>+128% organic clicks</strong><span>ThermoPest, six-month comparison</span></div>
        <div><strong>18+ month UK partnership</strong><span>Publicly reviewed by UK Auction List</span></div>
        <div><strong>GBP pricing available</strong><span>Calls in your morning, our afternoon</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section dark" id="why-us">
    <div class="container two-col">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Why UK businesses choose us</p>
        <h2>UK campaign delivery <span>that starts with the page holding you back</span></h2>
        <p>The numbers are public. Emily Bridalwear's organic clicks rose from 7.41k to 17.6k in a three-month, year-on-year comparison. ThermoPest's rose from 10.3k to 23.5k across two six-month periods. UK Auction List has publicly reviewed us after more than 18 months of partnership. The case studies and reviews are linked below.</p>
        <p class="lead">Every site has a part that is costing it enquiries: a catalogue shoppers cannot filter usefully, service pages that never say where you work, product comparisons that do not explain the differences. We find that part first and scope the campaign around fixing it.</p>
        <p>Before any content is commissioned we agree the priority URLs and the information we need from your team. For a UK store, delivery coverage, GBP prices and returns conditions matter as much as category demand. For a service business, the page must say what can be delivered and where. Responsibilities on both sides are written into the proposal.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card-grid small">
        <article class="card"><h3>Ecommerce brands</h3><p>Category and product page SEO, technical health and content that captures UK buying intent. Emily Bridalwear and Speediance UK are our published examples.</p></article>
        <article class="card"><h3>SaaS &amp; B2B</h3><p>Feature, comparison and use-case pages plus authority building for competitive UK software and services terms. Conscient is our published B2B case.</p></article>
        <article class="card"><h3>Service businesses</h3><p>Service and location pages for firms that win work through enquiries and bookings. ThermoPest and Phoenix Brighton Spa are our published cases.</p></article>
        <article class="card"><h3>UK agencies</h3><p>Fulfilment under your brand via our <a href="/white-label-seo-services-philippines/">white label SEO services</a>. Your agency owns the client conversation.</p></article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section darker" id="uk-results">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head centered">
        <p class="eyebrow">UK work and client reviews</p>
        <h2>UK project work, <span>published with its figures and its limits</span></h2>
        <p>The first four cards are Google Search Console comparisons for UK businesses, quoted exactly as the case studies report them. They measure search clicks, not sales. The Speediance card sets out the scope of a UK storefront project. The last three cards show the buying questions we review on sites like yours, each linked to one of our published AI-search reviews.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="results-grid">
        <article class="result-card">
          <p class="result-tag">Bridal retail · 3 months, year on year</p>
          <p class="result-metric">+137.5%</p>
          <h3>Emily Bridalwear: organic clicks</h3>
          <p>Clicks rose from 7.41k to 17.6k and impressions from 342k to 746k, comparing the last three months with the same period a year earlier. Average position improved from 24.8 to 14.9. The figures are rounded, and the screenshot does not show which queries gained.</p>
          <a href="/case-studies/bridal-dress-designer-seo/">Read the case study →</a>
        </article>
        <article class="result-card">
          <p class="result-tag">Pest control · 6-month comparison</p>
          <p class="result-metric">+128%</p>
          <h3>ThermoPest: organic clicks</h3>
          <p>Clicks rose from 10.3k to 23.5k and impressions from 1.26M to 3.12M across two six-month windows for pest.co.uk. Average position moved from 19.1 to 17.2 and click-through rate held at 0.8%. The comparison does not isolate which changes caused the difference.</p>
          <a href="/case-studies/pest-control-core-update-seo/">Read the case study →</a>
        </article>
        <article class="result-card">
          <p class="result-tag">B2B consultancy · 6-month comparison</p>
          <p class="result-metric">+19%</p>
          <h3>Conscient: organic clicks</h3>
          <p>Clicks rose from 13.2K to 15.7K and impressions from 398K to 742K, last six months against the previous six. Click-through rate fell from 3.3% to 2.1% as impressions grew. The screenshot records search performance, not a lead result.</p>
          <a href="/case-studies/consulting-firm-seo/">Read the case study →</a>
        </article>
        <article class="result-card">
          <p class="result-tag">Spa and beauty · 6-month comparison</p>
          <p class="result-metric">+70%</p>
          <h3>Phoenix Brighton Spa: organic clicks</h3>
          <p>Clicks rose from 5,570 to 9,480 and impressions from 145k to 418k. Average position got worse (17.8 to 21.5) and click-through rate fell from 3.8% to 2.3%. More search clicks do not establish more appointments, and the case study says so.</p>
          <a href="/case-studies/phoenix-spa-beauty-seo/">Read the case study →</a>
        </article>
        <article class="result-card">
          <p class="result-tag">Fitness Ecommerce</p>
          <p class="result-metric">UK</p>
          <h3>Speediance UK fitness ecommerce</h3>
          <p>Technical review, product search intent and content work for a UK home-gym storefront. The case study sets out the project scope and the campaign approach for this storefront in detail.</p>
          <a href="/case-studies/speediance-uk-fitness-seo/">Read the case study →</a>
        </article>
        <article class="result-card">
          <p class="result-tag">Review focus · AI search</p>
          <h3>Sports software: explain the product</h3>
          <p>A buyer of team-management software needs to compare squad administration, fixtures and reporting. We review whether each task has a clear page and a useful route into the product.</p>
          <a href="/case-studies/ai-sports-saas-geo/">Related AI-search review →</a>
        </article>
        <article class="result-card">
          <p class="result-tag">Review focus · AI search</p>
          <h3>Storage: answer the booking questions</h3>
          <p>Unit size, access hours, site location and booking conditions belong together. Our Newbury Self Store review looks at which pages AI referral visitors reached, before any decision to add location pages or expand the monitoring.</p>
          <a href="/case-studies/ai-self-storage-geo/">Related AI-search review →</a>
        </article>
        <article class="result-card">
          <p class="result-tag">Review focus · AI search</p>
          <h3>Bridal retail: support the appointment decision</h3>
          <p>Design ranges, sizing, stockists and appointment information answer different questions. We check where each detail belongs and how a shopper moves from a dress to an enquiry.</p>
          <a href="/case-studies/ai-bridal-ecommerce-geo/">Related AI-search review →</a>
        </article>
      </div>

      <div class="quote-grid">

        <figure class="quote-card">
          <blockquote>“We've been working with Ben and Bright Forge SEO for over 18 months, with the primary focus being our SEO needs. They deliver excellent results.”</blockquote>
          <figcaption><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/contrib/113689117491192336436/reviews?hl=en">UK Auction List, property auctions platform</a></figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure class="quote-card">
          <blockquote>“Working with Bright Forge SEO has been a huge benefit for TeamStats.”</blockquote>
          <figcaption><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/contrib/116331598386131030776/reviews?hl=en">Tom Stanbury, TeamStats (UK)</a></figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section dark" id="services">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head centered">
        <p class="eyebrow">What we deliver</p>
        <h2>Full-service search engine optimisation <span>for the UK market</span></h2>
        <p>Technical SEO, on-page work, content, link outreach and AI-search review, delivered by one team. Which comes first depends on your UK competitors, your existing pages and the changes your team can implement.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="service-grid">
        <article class="service-card"><i class="fas fa-search"></i><h3>SEO audits</h3><p>A review of technical problems, page coverage and relevant links, delivered as a list of affected URLs with the fixes in priority order.</p><a class="card-link" href="/seo-audit-services/">SEO audit services →</a></article>
        <article class="service-card"><i class="fas fa-sitemap"></i><h3>Technical SEO</h3><p>Crawl and indexation problems, site speed, schema and site structure, documented in written handovers your developer can implement and we then check on the live site.</p><a class="card-link" href="/technical-seo-services-philippines/">Technical SEO services →</a></article>
        <article class="service-card"><i class="fas fa-file-alt"></i><h3>On-page SEO</h3><p>Titles, headings, internal links and page structure revised to match how UK buyers search for what you sell.</p><a class="card-link" href="/on-page-seo-services-philippines/">On-page SEO services →</a></article>
        <article class="service-card"><i class="fas fa-pen-nib"></i><h3>Content SEO</h3><p>British English content mapped to UK search demand, buyer language and commercial intent, edited by a person before delivery.</p><a class="card-link" href="/content-seo-services-philippines/">Content SEO services →</a></article>
        <article class="service-card"><i class="fas fa-key"></i><h3>Keyword research</h3><p>UK demand mapped to specific pages, including the spelling, phrasing and intent differences that separate British searches from American ones.</p><a class="card-link" href="/keyword-research-services/">Keyword research →</a></article>
        <article class="service-card"><i class="fas fa-link"></i><h3>Link building &amp; digital PR</h3><p>Links from UK publications, directories and industry sites, chosen for relevance. Every prospect is reviewed before outreach begins.</p><a class="card-link" href="/backlink-seo-services-philippines/">Link building services →</a></article>
        <article class="service-card"><i class="fas fa-robot"></i><h3>AI Search Optimization</h3><p>Product and service facts, coverage of buyer questions and a defined sample of AI answers to monitor over agreed dates.</p><a class="card-link" href="/ai-search-optimization-services-philippines/">AI search services →</a></article>
        <article class="service-card"><i class="fas fa-map-marker-alt"></i><h3>Local SEO</h3><p>Business Profile, service pages and local information for UK businesses, built on the areas you really serve.</p><a class="card-link" href="/local-seo-for-google-maps-and-gbp/">Local SEO services →</a></article>
      </div>
      <p class="grid-note">Replatforming or redesigning? Our <a href="/website-migration-services/">website migration services</a> cover redirect mapping, launch checks and post-launch monitoring.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section darker" id="campaign">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head centered">
        <p class="eyebrow">Month by month</p>
        <h2>What a UK SEO campaign <span>looks like from month one to month twelve</span></h2>
        <p>This is an example sequence. Access, implementation effort and approvals set the actual delivery dates, and the phases describe work, not a schedule of ranking gains.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="phase-grid">
        <article class="phase-card">
          <p class="phase-step">Month 1</p>
          <h3>Diagnose</h3>
          <p>We review the site and the UK search results it competes in, confirm analytics and Search Console access and agree the priority pages with you. Fixes that need your developer are identified early so they can be scheduled.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="phase-card">
          <p class="phase-step">Months 2–3</p>
          <h3>Fix &amp; build</h3>
          <p>Approved technical and page changes are released, British English copy is reviewed and the agreed outreach begins. We check each live page after implementation.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="phase-card">
          <p class="phase-step">Months 4–6</p>
          <h3>Momentum</h3>
          <p>We compare query and landing-page performance with the baseline and check enquiries where the tracking is reliable. Where the work has not helped, the priorities change.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="phase-card">
          <p class="phase-step">Months 7–12</p>
          <h3>Review the next phase</h3>
          <p>We review coverage, competing pages and any changes in your offer. Then you decide whether to expand the campaign or concentrate on the problems still open.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
      <p class="grid-note">Every report shows the completed changes, a dated search comparison and the decisions still needed from your team. If you would also like a live dashboard, we can scope one through our <a href="/seo-command-centre/">SEO Command Centre</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section dark" id="pricing">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head centered">
        <p class="eyebrow">Pricing, honestly</p>
        <h2>How much do SEO services <span>cost in the UK?</span></h2>
        <p>We publish no rate card. Your proposal sets out the GBP fee and the work it covers. Each campaign type below needs different information from you before we can recommend a scope.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="price-table">
        <div class="price-row price-head">
          <span>Campaign type</span><span>Scope to agree</span><span>Information needed</span>
        </div>
        <div class="price-row">
          <span>Local &amp; small business SEO</span><span>Service-area review</span><span>Your actual service boundaries, the existing pages and whether the business is eligible for a Business Profile.</span>
        </div>
        <div class="price-row">
          <span>SME / national campaigns</span><span>Priority-page programme</span><span>The products or services to prioritise, your competitors and how much your team can implement.</span>
        </div>
        <div class="price-row">
          <span>B2B &amp; mid-market</span><span>Buyer and content research</span><span>The decision-makers, the questions sales hears and the subject experts available to review drafts.</span>
        </div>
        <div class="price-row">
          <span>Enterprise</span><span>Cross-team delivery plan</span><span>Regional ownership, approvals, technical dependencies and reporting requirements.</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="two-col pricing-cols">
        <div>
          <h3>What changes the price</h3>
          <ul class="tick-list">
            <li>How competitive your niche is on google.co.uk</li>
            <li>The size of the site and its accumulated technical debt</li>
            <li>The volume of content needed to close the gap</li>
            <li>Your authority deficit against the competitors that rank</li>
            <li>Whether the scope is local, national or international</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>What to bring to the scoping discussion</h3>
          <p>Send your website, your priority products or services, your target locations and the people available to review and implement changes. We use those details to separate the essential fixes from later expansion, so the first proposal covers only the work that has to happen first.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section darker" id="choose">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head centered">
        <p class="eyebrow">Buyer's guide</p>
        <h2>How to choose an SEO agency <span>in the UK</span></h2>
        <p>A fee tells you nothing about who does the work or what you keep at the end. Put these six checks to every agency on your shortlist. Each card ends with our own answer, so you can test us the same way.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="choose-grid">
        <article class="choose-card"><span class="choose-num">1</span><h3>Ask for named people</h3><p>Ask who researches, writes, implements and reviews, and which decisions need your approval. Our team is named on the about page, and Ben Lowe leads strategy and senior review.</p></article>
        <article class="choose-card"><span class="choose-num">2</span><h3>Ask for the numbers and their limits</h3><p>Ask for the starting point, the comparison period, the source and what the figure cannot show. Our Conscient case reports 19% more clicks and, beside it, a click-through rate that fell from 3.3% to 2.1%.</p></article>
        <article class="choose-card"><span class="choose-num">3</span><h3>Keep your own accounts</h3><p>GA4, Search Console and your website should live in your name. Walk away from any provider that insists on owning your data or your access. With us you keep full access throughout.</p></article>
        <article class="choose-card"><span class="choose-num">4</span><h3>Interrogate the links</h3><p>Ask for examples of the publications and directories proposed, why each is relevant and whether any placement carries a cost or an editorial condition. We review prospects for relevance before outreach and show you where every link comes from.</p></article>
        <article class="choose-card"><span class="choose-num">5</span><h3>Treat guarantees as red flags</h3><p>No provider controls rankings. Ask what will be delivered and how progress will be reviewed. Our proposal commits to named work and review points, never to a position.</p></article>
        <article class="choose-card"><span class="choose-num">6</span><h3>Read the exit terms</h3><p>Check the notice period, who owns commissioned work, the account access and the handover before signing. In our proposals the term, payment terms and notice period are written down beside the scope.</p></article>
      </div>
      <p class="grid-note">One more check for any team outside the UK: ask for its office hours in UK time. Ours run until 10:00 GMT or 11:00 BST, so live calls sit at the start of your morning and the rest is written handover. We do not present our Philippine team as a British office.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="pf-section" id="onshore-or-philippines">
    <div class="pf-wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">Two ways to buy</p>
      <h2>A UK agency or <span>a Philippines-based SEO team?</span></h2>
      <p class="pf-lede">Both can do good work. The differences are practical, so here they are side by side.</p>
      <div class="pf-table-wrap"><table class="pf-table">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Question</th><th scope="col">Onshore agency in the UK</th><th scope="col" class="pf-us">Bright Forge (Philippines-based team)</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">Working hours</th><td>Your business day, on UK time.</td><td class="pf-us">Monday to Friday, 9:00 to 18:00 Manila time: 01:00 to 10:00 GMT, or 02:00 to 11:00 BST.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Meetings</th><td>Video or in person. Workshops on your premises are possible.</td><td class="pf-us">Video calls in an agreed window. We have no office in the UK.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Market knowledge</th><td>Staff live in the UK and know its buyers first hand.</td><td class="pf-us">British-led, with published UK case studies. Your team supplies prices, delivery and returns details and approves regulated claims.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Who does the work</th><td>Varies. Some deliver in-house and some pass work to a white-label team, so ask.</td><td class="pf-us">The named team on our about page, based in Quezon City and British-led.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Evidence to ask for</th><td>Dated comparisons from their own clients, with the source and its limits.</td><td class="pf-us">Published UK comparisons for Emily Bridalwear, ThermoPest, Conscient and Phoenix Brighton Spa, each with its source and its limits.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Proposal and billing</th><td>Normally quoted in GBP.</td><td class="pf-us">A written, itemised proposal in GBP. We publish no rate card.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Best suited to</th><td>Buyers who need people in the room or on site.</td><td class="pf-us">Buyers comfortable with scheduled calls and written handovers.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>
      <p class="pf-note">A general comparison of two ways of buying SEO. It describes trade-offs and makes no claim about any particular agency.</p>
